## Source boundary and matching intent

The [Act 3 concept](../../../docs/ACT3_CONCEPT.md#three-regular-enemy-roles) requires two crest shapes identifying the sun state and easier flank, with ordinary slash recovery in both states. [C50's entity record](../../../docs/reference-library/act3/research/entities.json) identifies an invented game creature; scenic sun state does not change the recovery hitbox. The fork is root-authored original game art, not novel canon, an exclusive attack side or a new organ.

Family vocabulary follows [G12's selected regular-threat study](../../../docs/concept-art/act3/bosses/03-candidates-and-threats.png) and [G18's Twin Suns gameplay board](../../../docs/concept-art/act3/gameplay/01-twin-suns-gameplay.png), through the existing native cutouts. Violet angular organic plates, four bent limbs, lowered head and restrained ivory/lime seam remain the family. G18's line remains a committed lunge lane, not a laser. [The original Branchspell record](stalker-production.md) retains the earlier source and selected-board provenance.

Actually opened both local input PNGs immediately before the edit, and the generated native PNG afterward. The settled recovery was the exact body/pose target. Root selected the stronger front Alppain fork for an actual consumer trial only; this recovery is a candidate mate to that configuration. The prompt asked for only the same short broad open head fork, preserving low recovery posture, four limbs, y1060 baseline and native size. It requested a widest opening at least 58 pixels, preferably 60–90.

## Native anatomy, contour drift and gap measurement

The output visibly retains the low settled torso, deeply bent bracing legs, four clawed foot ends/limb family, wide planted stance, lower-right head direction, original dorsal plate arrangement and pale ivory/muted-lime low flank join. The head-side crest becomes a broad short angular two-prong fork with an open notch; there is no added organism, floor, contact shadow, glow outline, equipment, attack/cue shape, target gem, text or atlas.

It is not an exact crest-only pixel patch. Facets and some fine limb/foot edges were redrawn. Relative to the original recovery's alpha-128 bounds `[129,416,1153,1060]`, the top moves three pixels up to y413, lowest foot bound two pixels down to y1062, left edge one pixel left and right edge two pixels right. Width increases from 1024 to 1027 and height from 644 to 649. At unchanged family scale the height difference is about 0.008364 world units and the below-anchor foot edge about 0.003346 world units. The low recovery identity remains recognizable, but registration/floor contact is not accepted from these bounds.

Read-only alpha inspection distinguishes the space above the fork lips from the opening between the two prongs:

- In broad head ROI `[820,610,1101,781]`, the largest alpha<128 run horizontally bounded by visible anatomy is **68 pixels**, x897→965 at y613. This upper-mouth measurement includes the neighbouring head/back contour above the shorter fork lip; it does not prove a 68-pixel usable opening between both prongs.
- Restricting to the paired-prong region `[820,625,1101,761]` yields a widest bounded opening of **51 pixels**, x927→978 at y701. The same maximum persists for y630–760 and y645–745. Sampled rows y630–750 taper from 28 through 31/38/47/50/48/40/27 to 13 pixels.

Therefore the requested **at least 58-pixel gap between both substantial prongs is not established**; this candidate retains a narrower central opening than the selected braced fork's measured 58 pixels. The upper opening and deeper notch may still make a useful paired silhouette, but the prompt criterion must not be marked met by using the broader neighbouring-contour run. At the prior source-still horizontal scale of roughly 0.074–0.078 portrait pixels per native pixel, a 51-pixel central opening would be about 3.8–4.0 pixels. That is an estimate from earlier braced-source captures, not a GPU measurement of this new recovery PNG. No retry or corrective processing was performed.

